#X text 240 83 Generates a Euclidean rhythm: distributes a given number
of beats as evenly as possible across a sequence of a given length.
For example \, 3 beats over 8 steps produce the classic "x..x..x."
pattern. An offset shifts the entire pattern by the given number of
steps.;
#X text 240 168 Euclidean rhythms are widely used in electronic music
and algorithmic composition to obtain natural-sounding \, well-balanced
rhythmic patterns from just two numbers — the number of beats and the
total length. Many traditional rhythms (for example \, the bossa nova
clave or various African bell patterns) are Euclidean and can be reproduced
with this object.;
#X text 240 284 Note: a fast Bresenham-based algorithm is used internally
\, so even long sequences (up to 8192 steps) are computed quickly.;
